Resolve a dangling link's relative target from the link's directory.
When a link's target didn't exist, xmalloc_realpath_coreutils() made a
relative target absolute by prefixing the current directory, but the
system resolves it from the directory that holds the link. For a link
outside the current directory, as in "ln -s missing dir/link",
"readlink -f dir/link" and "realpath dir/link" printed /CURDIR/missing
instead of /CURDIR/dir/missing. Prefix the link's directory instead.
For a link with no directory part the target is left as it is, and a
bare name is resolved against the current directory, which the earlier
change for names without a directory part provides.

---
 libbb/xreadlink.c |   18 ++++++++++--------
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 8 deletions(-)

--- a/libbb/xreadlink.c
+++ b/libbb/xreadlink.c
@@ -164,15 +164,17 @@
 			if (target[0] != '/') {
 #endif
 				/*
-				 * $ ln -s target_does_not_exist symlink
-				 * $ readlink -f symlink
-				 * /CURDIR/target_does_not_exist
-				 * $ realpath symlink
-				 * /CURDIR/target_does_not_exist
+				 * $ ln -s target_does_not_exist dir/symlink
+				 * $ readlink -f dir/symlink
+				 * /CURDIR/dir/target_does_not_exist
+				 * $ realpath dir/symlink
+				 * /CURDIR/dir/target_does_not_exist
 				 */
-				char *cwd = xrealloc_getcwd_or_warn(NULL);
-				char *tmp = concat_path_file(cwd, target);
-				free(cwd);
+				char *dir = xstrdup(path);
+				char *tmp;
+				*bb_get_last_path_component_strip(dir) = '\0';
+				tmp = concat_path_file(dir, target);
+				free(dir);
 				free(target);
 				target = tmp;
 			}
